# M-readable-bytes' other direction, panel 164. A header field's ADDRESS
# crosses to C, so C may read the bytes with a length beside them and write
# back into them.
#
# WHAT IT COST TO REACH, and the sitting's premise turned out to be false. Defect
# 061 said in bold that *no Heroes-side shim can route around it*, and the brief
# carried that sentence as the question. Three seats ran it and it is wrong:
# `f.validated_bytes().must().cstr()` reaches a `cstr` parameter today and, on an
# eight-byte field with no terminator, hands C eight bytes where C's own decay
# hands it seventeen. The true sentence is narrower — no shim lends the field's
# ADDRESS — and it is what this case is about.
#
# WHY `ptr` AND NEVER `cstr`. Four seats vetoed the decay and two vetoed a `cstr`
# lend, on one ground: a `cstr` is a promise of a terminating zero, design.md
# §1.11 lists that promise among the decisions not to revisit, and a header's
# `char[N]` field makes no such promise — 37 of 50 such fields across 16 headers
# have none. So the text route stays `validated_bytes`, which COPIES, and this
# lends the field itself to a `ptr` parameter that was told the extent.
#
# THE WRITE IS THE HALF THAT HAD NO DOOR, and it is why this case reads the
# field back after C has filled it. The ffi seat measured that 50 of 141 pointer
# parameters across those headers are non-`const` — `getcwd`, `read` — and
# nothing in the language could produce their argument.
#
# AND THE ASSERTION AFTER THE FILL IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE CASE. The first
# implementation rendered the lend from `emit/storageless.hero`, which is right
# for `validated_bytes` and wrong here: the IR loads a record into a fresh
# temporary before reading a field of it, so C was handed the address of a COPY.
# Reading through it was correct by accident; writing through it was lost in
# silence, at exit 0, with every suite green. A case that stopped at "it compiles
# and runs" would have blessed that.

extern "ffi-a-byte-field-crosses-to-c.h"
    record Slot
        nsap: u8[8]
        tag: i8[4]
        id: i64
    function slot_sum(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64
    function slot_fill(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64)

function main()
    s: Slot @ Slot(nsap: [1, 0, 3, 0, 5, 0, 7, 0], tag: [9, 8, 7, 6], id: 0)

    # C reads the field, told where it ends by the argument beside it. The
    # zeros inside are data here, not a terminator, which is the case a
    # terminator-reading route gets wrong.
    print(slot_sum(p: s.nsap.ptr(), n: 8))

    # Both signs cross, because the HEADER decides the element type and the
    # binding author cannot — M-arm-platform's finding, met again.
    print(slot_sum(p: s.tag.ptr(), n: 4))

    # C writes into the field, and the program reads it back. 1+2+...+8.
    slot_fill(p: s.nsap.ptr(), n: 8)
    print(slot_sum(p: s.nsap.ptr(), n: 8))
    print(s.nsap[3].to_i64().must())

    # The other direction is untouched and still copies: a terminated run of
    # the same kind of field is text.
    t: Slot @ Slot(nsap: [72, 105, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0], tag: [0, 0, 0, 0], id: 1)
    print(t.nsap.validated_bytes().must())
